# Cursor rules — Ryngo project map

This repository is mapped by Ryngo (https://ryngo.ai). The `.ryngo/`
directory at the repo root is your structural source of truth.

When the user asks for a structural change:

1. **Read `.ryngo/intents/` first.** Most "I want this changed" requests
   already have a stub there with affected nodes listed. If you find a
   matching intent, work against it; don't invent a parallel plan.

2. **Honor `.ryngo/rules.md`.** It contains hard architectural
   invariants ("auth must not import payments", etc.). Treat as
   non-negotiable.

3. **Reference nodes by Ryngo id.** When you discuss code in chat or in
   commit messages, use the stable ids from Ryngo (`file:…`, `def:…`,
   `cell:…`, `pkg:…`) so the user can map your reply onto the graph.

4. **Update intent status.** When you complete an intent, change the
   frontmatter `status:` field from `open` to `done` in the same commit.

5. **Don't rewrite `.ryngo/`** unless the user explicitly asks. The
   files there are user-authored or Ryngo-authored — append, don't
   overwrite.

6. **Annotations are guidance, not requirements.** Notes in
   `.ryngo/annotations.md` are hints and TODOs. Surface them in your
   plan when relevant; don't act on them silently.

After your change lands, Ryngo will re-analyse the repo and tick the
acceptance checklist on each intent. Aim for every box to tick.
